
===== story:bio (18s, 2238 chars)
Zander Demo is a former Operations Director who grew the HVAC division at Harlow & Pike from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years. He now helps MDs in building services get real visibility on where the margin is going and get out of the operational middle.

- **£4.2m to £11.8m** HVAC division revenue growth at Harlow & Pike over five years
- **£1,950/month** Minimum engagement fee
- **8** Maximum clients carried at any one time

### Proof inventory

| Result | Where | Timeframe | How | Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Revenue grew from £4.2m to £11.8m | HVAC division, Harlow & Pike Building Services | 5 years | Shifted model from one-off installs to planned maintenance contracts; tightened job pricing to hold margin through growth | Ready |

### Short bio

I spent fifteen years in building services, starting as a Project Engineer and working up to Operations Director at Harlow & Pike. During that time I grew the HVAC division from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years by moving the business off one-off installs and onto planned maintenance contracts, and by tightening how we priced and tracked jobs so margin held through the growth. I now work with MDs and owners in M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting who are winning work but can't explain why the profit isn't following. Eight clients at a time. I get into the numbers from week one.

### One-line introduction

Zander Demo is a former Operations Director in building services who helps M&E and HVAC contractors get real visibility on job margin and get the MD out of the weeds.

### For partners and events

Zander Demo spent fifteen years in building services, progressing from Project Engineer to Contracts Manager to Operations Director at Harlow & Pike Building Services. He grew the Harlow & Pike HVAC division from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years by shifting the model from one-off installs to planned maintenance contracts and holding margin through disciplined job pricing. He now coaches MDs and owners in M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting firms turning over £2m to £12m, helping them get real-time visibility on job margin, get unpaid retentions moving, and get out of the operational middle. He carries a maximum of eight clients and works hybrid from Leeds.

===== market:options (25s, 3069 chars)
Zander's strongest market is HVAC and M&E contracting firms, where his Harlow & Pike proof is exact, the pain is acute, and owners will not need convincing he understands the industry.

| Option | Value | Pain | Growing | Easy to find | Purchasing power |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| HVAC and M&E contracting (£1m–£15m) | 5 | 5 | 4 | 4 | 4 |
| Planned maintenance and FM contractors (£2m–£20m) | 4 | 4 | 4 | 3 | 4 |
| Electrical contracting (£1m–£10m) | 3 | 5 | 4 | 3 | 3 |

### Option 1: HVAC and M&E contracting firms (£1m–£15m)

This is the exact world Zander operated in. The Harlow & Pike result, £4.2m to £11.8m in five years by tightening job pricing and shifting to planned maintenance contracts, is specific proof in a specific sector. The MD reading it will recognise the problems immediately: margin disappearing on jobs that looked profitable at tender stage, retentions sitting unpaid for months, and the owner still answering every site call at 7am. Zander does not need to earn credibility here. He starts with it. MDs in this sector are sceptical of generic coaches and that scepticism is his advantage, because he removes the objection before it is raised.

### Option 2: Planned maintenance and FM contractors (£2m–£20m)

Facilities management and planned maintenance businesses share most of the same pains: owner dependency, retention cash, and margin erosion on recurring contracts. Zander understands this model from the inside because the shift to planned maintenance contracts was the growth lever he pulled at Harlow & Pike. The proof is not as direct as Option 1, but it is relevant and credible. The businesses are slightly harder to find than pure M&E firms, and the buying decision can involve more stakeholders in larger FM operations. A solid second market, particularly once Option 1 is established.

### Option 3: Electrical contracting businesses (£1m–£10m)

Electrical contractors face the same cash flow and margin problems as M&E and HVAC firms, and Zander has sector knowledge here. The issue is proof. There is no specific electrical contracting result to anchor the conversation. Owners in this sector are often price-sensitive and will ask for evidence that the fee is worth it. Without a named result from electrical contracting, Zander is selling credibility he has to explain rather than demonstrate. Viable as a secondary market, weaker as a starting point.

### Recommended core client

> My core client is an MD or owner of an HVAC or M&E contracting firm with 15 to 40 employees doing £1M to £15M revenue, who is winning work but can't explain why the profit isn't following.

> **If Options 1 and 2 feel equally strong on the Decision Call:** Ask yourself one question: which MD would feel immediately understood in the first sixty seconds of a conversation, without Zander having to explain the industry context? The answer is the HVAC or M&E contractor. Start there, build the proof record, and open Option 2 as a second lane once the first three or four clients are in place and the case studies are real.

===== market:avatar (55s, 7422 chars)
Margin-Blind Mike: MD of a 25-person M&E contracting firm in West Yorkshire, turning over £4m a year and still not sure where half the profit goes.

- **Name:** Mike
- **Age:** 42 to 54
- **Location:** West Yorkshire or East Midlands, within an hour of a main contractor hub
- **Business:** M&E or HVAC contracting firm, typically second-generation or founder-built
- **Revenue:** £2m to £12m
- **Team:** 15 to 40 people: engineers, site supervisors, office staff, one or two contracts managers
- **Role:** MD or Owner - the person whose name is on the building
- **Time in the business:** 10 to 25 years, started on the tools or came up through the commercial side

### A day in the life

It is 7.14am and Mike is already on his second call. One of the lads on the Sheffield scheme has a query about a riser, something the contracts manager could handle, but the contracts manager rang Mike first because that is how it works. Mike answers it, makes a mental note to talk to Dave about stepping up, and pulls into the car park. By 8am he has two emails from a main contractor chasing a programme update, a WhatsApp from a subbie about a delayed delivery, and a reminder from his accountant about a VAT return he needs to look at. He sits down at his desk with a coffee and opens the job tracker. The numbers are three weeks behind. He scrolls through the contracts on the board and picks out the Wakefield job, the one the lads were flat out on all summer. Final account came back last week. He expected to see around £60k net. The number on screen is £18k. He stares at it for a moment, opens a spreadsheet, closes it again. He has a site visit at ten, a call with a client at noon, and a pricing meeting at three. He does not have time to go through the job line by line. He tells himself he will look at it properly at the weekend. He has been saying that for six weeks. Somewhere between the VAT reminder and the second WhatsApp from the subbie, it occurs to him that he has worked harder this year than any year before it, and he will probably take less home.

### In his words

> The Wakefield job should have been a decent earner. Lads were busy all summer, no major issues on site, variations got submitted. And then the final account comes in and there's barely owt left. I've looked at it three times and I still can't put my finger on it. Was it the materials? Did we underprice the second fix? Did the variations get absorbed somewhere? I don't know. And that's the bit that gets me - I should know. I've been doing this long enough. Dave's good, but he still rings me for stuff I thought he'd be handling by now. I don't blame him, that's just how it's always worked. The phone goes and I answer it. I had four days off in July and it didn't stop. Not once. I keep thinking if I just had a bit more time I'd get on top of it, sort the pricing out, get the retentions chased, maybe look at how we structure the team. But the time never comes. And in the meantime the turnover keeps going up and I'm not sure any of it is actually coming through to me. Something's not adding up and I don't really know where to start.
>
> Mike, MD, M&E contracting firm, West Yorkshire

### What they want

- To know, in real time, whether a job is making or losing money - before the final account lands and it is too late to fix anything.
- To stop being the first call for every site query, materials sign-off and client complaint, and to trust the contracts manager to handle what he should be handling.
- To get the retentions moving - tens of thousands sitting in holdbacks from jobs that closed over a year ago, and someone accountable for chasing every one of them.
- To take a proper week off without the phone going, knowing the business will not fall apart while he is away.
- To see the revenue growth he has put in actually convert into money in his pocket at the end of the year.

### What they fear

- That the margin bleed is worse than he thinks, and when he finally looks properly the numbers will be genuinely bad.
- That the business only works because he is always in it, and if he stepped back for a month it would start to unravel.
- That one of his key engineers or supervisors hands in their notice and half the operational knowledge walks out with them.
- That he gets to 55 and realises he has built something he cannot step back from, cannot sell for what it is worth, and cannot leave.
- That a VAT bill or a bad month lands in the same window as three retentions still sitting unpaid, and the cash position suddenly looks very tight.

### What they have tried

- Brought in a consultant who spent three months doing a review, produced a report Mike already agreed with, and left without fixing anything.
- Promoted the contracts manager and waited for things to improve. Dave is better than he was, but the calls still come through to Mike.
- Set up a job tracker in a spreadsheet that three people use differently and nobody updates on time.
- Told himself he would sort the pricing properly after the next busy period. That was two busy periods ago.
- Paid an accountant to run monthly management accounts that arrive six weeks late and tell him what already happened, not what is happening now.

### What they suspect

- That most business coaches have never run a live operation and will spend the first few months asking him to explain what a retention is.
- That any system a consultant brings will be built for a different type of business and will need so much adapting it creates more work than it solves.
- That the fee will be easy to justify to themselves but hard to defend to the business if it does not produce something tangible fast.
- That the honest answer to most of these problems is things they already half-know, and what they really need is someone to get specific and hold them to it.

### What finally makes them act

- A final account comes in £50k to £80k short on a job that looked solid at tender stage, and Mike cannot explain the gap.
- A retention from a job that closed eighteen months ago gets written off because nobody was chasing it and the main contractor has moved on.
- A trusted site supervisor or engineer hands in their notice and Mike realises how much of the day-to-day operation lives inside that one person's head.
- He sits down and works out what he took home last year against the hours he put in. The number shocks him.
- A holiday or a family commitment gets cancelled because a site issue needs him personally, and someone close to him says something he cannot unhear.

### How they talk

| Thing | What they call it |
| --- | --- |
| The people they work for | The client, the main contractor, the MC |
| Their own workforce | The lads, the engineers, the site team |
| Individual contracts | Jobs, schemes, contracts |
| Subcontractors | Subbies |
| Unpaid holdbacks | Retentions, retention money, holdbacks |
| Invoices submitted for payment | Applications, apps, valuations |
| Disputed or additional scope | Variations, vars, extras |
| Closing out a job financially | Final account, the FA |
| Profit on a contract | Margin, what we made on the job |
| Pricing new work | Tendering, putting a price in, quoting |

### Ready to act

> I'm done finding out six months after the fact that a job lost money. I want to know at week four, when I can still do something about it. And I want to take a week off without the phone going.
>
> Mike

===== story:onepager (36s, 4247 chars)
Zander Demo is a former Operations Director in building services who grew the HVAC division at Harlow & Pike from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years. He now works with MDs and owners of M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting firms who are winning work but can't explain why the profit isn't following.

- **£4.2m to £11.8m** HVAC division revenue at Harlow & Pike, over five years
- **£1,950/month** Minimum engagement fee
- **8** Maximum clients carried at any one time

### Who I help

My core client is the MD or owner of an M&E, HVAC or electrical contracting firm turning over £2m to £12m, with 15 to 40 people on the books. Usually founder-built or second-generation. The person whose name is on the building. They are winning more work than ever, the lads are busy, and yet the money at the end of the year does not reflect what they put in. They cannot tell you exactly why. They are the first call for every site query, every client complaint, every decision Dave should be making himself. They have not had a proper week off in years. They know something is not adding up and they do not know where to start.

### The problems I fix

- Margin bleed: jobs close and the final account is short, sometimes by £50k or more, and nobody can say where it went or catch it early enough to do anything about it.
- Owner dependency: the MD is still the escalation point for every site call, every sign-off, every problem the contracts manager should be handling, and there are no hours left for the work that actually moves the business forward.
- Retentions sitting idle: tens of thousands in holdbacks from jobs that closed over a year ago, with no one accountable for chasing them and the cash just sitting there.
- No early warning: by the time the numbers show a job is in trouble, the margin is gone and the damage is done.

### How I work

1. **First conversation.** A straightforward call to understand what is happening in the business right now: which jobs are causing concern, where the cash is tight, what the MD cannot step away from. We complete the BOSS Scorecard together so we both know which area is capping the business before we start.
2. **Into the numbers from week one.** I sit in on job reviews and pricing discussions early. That is where margin bleed shows up and where owner dependency is most visible. I do not need six months learning how the industry works. Weekly sessions in the first four to six weeks to land early wins on margin and cash.
3. **A rhythm that holds.** Two 90-minute sessions a month, ongoing. A weekly numbers rhythm the team can run without the MD in the room. Retentions tracked, jobs flagged early, decisions sitting at the right level. Direct access between sessions for anything that cannot wait. Hybrid: calls on Tuesdays and Wednesdays, site or office visits where they add value.

### Results

- Grew the HVAC division at Harlow & Pike from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years by shifting the model from one-off installs to planned maintenance contracts and tightening job pricing to hold margin through the growth.
- Fifteen years inside building services as Project Engineer, Contracts Manager and Operations Director: the same margin, retention and owner-dependency problems I now help contractors fix are problems I have lived through and resolved in a live operation.

### Working together

If you work with MDs or owners in M&E, HVAC or electrical contracting, the clients worth introducing are those turning over £2m to £12m who are growing in revenue but not in profit, who are still the operational centre of their business, or who are sitting on unpaid retentions with no clear process to recover them. These are not businesses in crisis. They are businesses that have outgrown how they were running and need someone who already knows the industry to get into the numbers alongside them. The engagement starts at £1,950 a month and I carry a maximum of eight clients, so introductions get proper attention. A warm introduction from you means the MD does not have to spend the first conversation convincing me what a final account is.

### Working together

- **LinkedIn:** linkedin.com/in/zander-demo
- **Website:** Profit Coach page (details on request)
- **Location:** Leeds, West Yorkshire

===== voice:sound (29s, 3293 chars)
Zander talks like someone who has stood on the same sites his clients are standing on. He is direct, unhurried, and specific, with a dry Northern matter-of-factness that makes him instantly credible to an MD who has been burned by polished consultants before.

- **Tone:** Straight-talking, grounded, dry, specific, no-nonsense
- **Pace:** Measured. He takes his time to say the right thing, not the quick thing.
- **Formality:** Low. First names from the off. No corporate language, no coaching jargon.
- **Humour:** Dry and occasional. A wry observation rather than a punchline. Never at the client's expense.
- **Regional flavour:** Yorkshire. 'Aye', 'nowt', 'lads', 'flat out'. Not performed, just natural.

### Sounds like

- 'They were winning work, lads were busy all summer, and the final account came back with nowt left. That's not bad luck. That's a pricing and tracking problem, and it'll happen again on the next job if nobody gets into the numbers.'
- 'Eight clients at a time. That's it. I want to be properly in the business, not just jumping on a call once a fortnight and calling it coaching.'
- 'I've lived through margin bleed, retentions sitting unpaid, and an MD who's the bottleneck for everything. I'm not reading that out of a textbook.'

### Does not sound like

- 'I help visionary leaders unlock sustainable growth by leveraging their operational excellence.' (Too polished, too vague, zero credibility with a site-level MD.)
- 'Let's unpack your journey and align your strategy with your core values.' (Coaching cliche. Would make Mike put the phone down.)
- 'I'm passionate about empowering business owners to achieve their full potential.' (Generic. Sounds like every other coach. Zander is the opposite of generic.)'

### Words and phrases to use

- Aye, nowt, lads, flat out
- Jobs, schemes, contracts
- Subbies, main contractor, MC
- Retentions, holdbacks, retention money
- Final account, the FA
- Margin, what we made on the job
- Variations, vars, extras
- Tendering, putting a price in
- Applications, apps, valuations
- Get into the numbers
- Weekly rhythm
- Early warning
- Stuck in the middle
- Out of the weeds
- Properly in the business
- That's not bad luck, that's a [specific problem]

### Words to avoid

- Empower, unlock, elevate
- Leverage (as a verb)
- Robust, seamless, holistic
- Journey, pathway, transformation
- Unpack, dive deep, navigate
- Passionate, visionary, thought leader
- Synergy, alignment, ecosystem
- In today's world, now more than ever
- Business coaching (say 'working with' or name the specific problem instead)

### A message in your voice

**Example**

```text
Hi Mike, I spent fifteen years in building services, the last few as Operations Director at Harlow & Pike, where we grew the HVAC division from £4.2m to £11.8m. Most of that came down to fixing the same things I see in most M&E firms: margin bleeding out on jobs without anyone catching it early, retentions sitting unpaid for months, and the MD still fielding calls he shouldn't be. I work with eight contractors at a time and I get into the numbers from week one. Worth a conversation?
```

_Use on LinkedIn connection requests or first direct messages to MDs and owners in M&E, HVAC or electrical contracting. Keep it as is, no softening, no extra sell._

