I now work with MDs and owners in M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting who are in the same position. Typically turning over £2m to £12m, 15 to 40 people, and busy - but the profit isn't following the revenue the way it should.

This is the right fit if:
- You've had a final account land short and you still can't say exactly where the margin went
- You've got retention money sitting out there from jobs that closed over a year ago with nobody properly chasing it
- Your contracts manager is decent but the phone still comes through to you for decisions it shouldn't
- You worked harder last year than any year before it and took less home

Here's how I work. In the first four to six weeks I get into the job reviews and pricing discussions. Not a workshop, not a framework - I sit in the numbers with you and find where the margin is going before it bleeds out completely. Then we build a weekly rhythm that keeps it visible. You see problems at week four, when there's still time to do something, not at the final account when the damage is done.

I carry eight clients at a time. That's it. Each one gets proper time and someone who already knows what a retention looks like, how a variation gets priced, and what happens when the MC starts squeezing the programme.

At Harlow & Pike, the same problems your business has right now were the ones we fixed. The proof is exact, not approximate.

