===== offer:direction (41s, 5529 chars)
I help MDs in M&E and HVAC contracting get real visibility on where the margin is going and get out of the operational middle, without spending months explaining how the industry works, using a weekly numbers rhythm built around job costing, retentions, and pricing.

### The gap

| Today | What they want |
| --- | --- |
| Final accounts landing £40k to £80k short with no clear explanation | Know at week four whether a job is making or losing money, when there is still time to act |
| Retentions sitting unpaid for months, some written off entirely, nobody accountable | One person responsible for every holdback, on a set schedule, with the cash moving |
| MD fielding every site call, signing off every decision, working holidays that never happen | Contracts manager and site team making their own calls, MD stepping in only when it genuinely needs him |
| Management accounts arriving six weeks late, decisions made on gut feel | A weekly numbers rhythm showing what is happening now, not what happened last month |

### Why they will buy

- Know-how: Mike suspects most coaches have never stood on a live site. Zander spent fifteen years in building services, finishing as Operations Director. He already knows what a retention looks like, how a variation gets absorbed, and what happens when an MC squeezes the programme. There is no warm-up period.
- Speed: Zander gets into job reviews and pricing discussions from week one. Mike does not spend the first three months explaining how contracting works. The weekly rhythm starts immediately and early warning replaces late surprises.
- Ease: The rhythm is built around how M&E contracting actually runs, job costing, applications, retentions, final accounts. Mike recognises it immediately. There is no generic framework to adapt or a consultant's report to file and forget.
- Fit: This is not a coach who works across every sector. Zander only takes eight clients. Mike is the exact person he built this for: MD of a contracting firm, winning work, not banking what he expected, and tired of being the bottleneck.

### Key messages

1. You are winning work. The lads are busy. The revenue is up. So why is the profit not following? Margin bleed in contracting is almost never bad luck. It is a job costing and tracking problem, and it repeats on every similar scheme until someone gets into the numbers and fixes the root cause.
2. The phone goes and you answer it. That is not a personnel problem, it is a structural one. Until there is a clear rhythm of accountability below you, the calls will keep coming through to you, including on the days you are supposed to be off.
3. Retention money is not a bonus. It is earned revenue sitting idle, sometimes for eighteen months, sometimes written off entirely because nobody with clear accountability was chasing it. In a tight month that gap is the difference between comfortable and scrambling.
4. Six-week-old management accounts tell you what already happened. By the time the numbers land, you cannot change anything. A weekly rhythm built around live job data gives you sight of a problem in time to do something about it.
5. The Wakefield job, the Sheffield job, the one before that. If you cannot explain where the margin went, the same thing will happen on the next job. The answer is not more hours. It is a pricing and tracking process that holds margin from tender to final account.

### The offer

- **Name:** The Contractor's Profit Rhythm
- **Promise:** Real visibility on job margin, unpaid retentions moving, and the MD out of the operational middle, within the first ninety days.
- **Who it is for:** MDs and owners of M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting firms turning over £2m to £12m. Founder-led or family businesses where the owner is still the bottleneck. Not start-ups. Not anyone under £1m. Not anyone who wants a report and no follow-through.
- **What is included:** Weeks one to four: Zander sits in on job reviews and pricing discussions, diagnoses where margin is bleeding, and sets the baseline numbers rhythm. From week five: two 90-minute sessions a month, focused on job costing, retentions tracking, team accountability and pricing. Zander stays close to the live operation, not just the strategy. Hybrid delivery so there is real site-level context where it is useful.
- **Format:** Weekly sessions for the first four to six weeks, then two 90-minute sessions a month on Tuesdays or Wednesdays. Eight clients maximum. Hybrid.
- **Price options:** £1,950 per month rolling. Six months upfront available at a reduced rate. Price rises by £500 every two to four clients as capacity fills. Prospect calls on Mondays and Thursdays from 1pm.

### Why you

- Grew the HVAC division at Harlow & Pike from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years by shifting from one-off installs to planned maintenance contracts and holding margin through disciplined job pricing.
- Spent fifteen years in building services: Project Engineer at Northern M&E, Contracts Manager and then Operations Director at Harlow & Pike Building Services.
- Has lived through margin bleed, retentions sitting unpaid, and an MD who is the bottleneck for everything. Not reading it from a textbook.
- Carries a maximum of eight clients. Gets properly into the numbers from week one, not jumping on a call once a fortnight and calling it coaching.
- No onboarding period. Knows what a retention looks like, how a variation gets priced, and what it costs when an MC squeezes the programme. Clients in this sector recognise it immediately.

===== offer:payment (38s, 4666 chars)
The fee is not a cost. It is what staying in the same position for another year actually costs Mike. Frame it that way from the first conversation.

| Option | Investment | Best for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Monthly rolling | £1,950 per month | MDs who want to start, see results in the first 90 days, and build trust before committing further |
| Six months upfront (single payment) | £10,200 | MDs who are ready to commit and want one clean decision with no monthly admin |
| Six months upfront (two instalments) | £5,400 now, £5,400 at month three | MDs who want the certainty of a fixed programme but prefer to split the outlay across the period |
| Six months upfront (50% now, then monthly) | £5,100 now, then £1,950 per month to month six | MDs who want to lock in the engagement but keep some cash flexibility in the early months |

### If they ask the price early

**Price anchor script**

```text
The MDs I work with invest up to £50,000 a year when they're deep into it. But you can get started for £1,950 a month. What it actually costs depends on where you are and what we're trying to fix. I'm on prospect calls Monday and Thursday afternoons - I could do a call Monday at one or Thursday at one and we'd know inside twenty minutes whether it makes sense. Which suits you better?
```

_Use this any time price comes up before you have had a proper conversation. It anchors high, brings it back down to a credible entry point, and moves straight to booking the call. Do not elaborate on the pricing structure until you are on the decision call._

### At the end of the call

**Either-or close**

```text
Right, based on what you've told me, the starting point is getting a weekly numbers rhythm in place around the job costing and the retentions - that's where the early wins are. Most MDs I work with start on the monthly rolling at £1,950, keeps it clean. Some prefer to put six months in upfront and get it done. Which suits you better?
```

_Ask this after you have agreed the problem and the plan. Two options only. Do not offer all four at once. If they want time to think, give them a specific follow-up: 'I'll send you a one-pager today. Can we speak Thursday at one to confirm?'_

### Proposal outline

1. **Where you are now.** One short paragraph reflecting what Mike told you on the call. Name the specific jobs, the retention figure, the hours, the number that shocked him. This section should feel like you were listening, not like a template. If he mentioned the Wakefield job, it goes here.
2. **What it is costing.** Put a number on the problem where you can. Margin bleeding on two or three jobs a year at £20k to £60k a time. Retentions sitting unpaid. Hours spent on calls that should sit below him. Make the cost of doing nothing visible.
3. **What we are going to do.** The Contractor's Profit Rhythm. Weeks one to four: Zander sits in on job reviews and pricing discussions, sets the baseline numbers rhythm, identifies where the margin is going. From week five: two 90-minute sessions a month on Tuesdays or Wednesdays, focused on job costing, retentions, team accountability and pricing. Hybrid. Eight clients maximum.
4. **What you can expect in the first 90 days.** Real-time visibility on which jobs are making money and which are not. At least one retention moving that was sitting idle. A clear picture of where margin bled on the last three jobs and a pricing process to stop it repeating. The contracts manager handling a defined set of decisions without it coming back to Mike.
5. **Why this works.** Grew the HVAC division at Harlow & Pike from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years by fixing the same problems: job pricing, margin bleed, and owner dependency. Fifteen years in building services. Gets into the numbers from week one. No onboarding period, no warm-up. Eight clients at a time.
6. **Your investment and next step.** Monthly rolling at £1,950 per month, or six months upfront. Present the two options you discussed on the call. Include a single clear next step: a link to pay or a date to confirm. Keep this paragraph short.

### Raising your price

Start at £1,950 per month and raise by £500 every two to four clients as capacity fills. At four clients, move to £2,450. At six, move to £2,950. At eight, you are full. New clients at that point join a waiting list or pay the current rate, whichever you set. If a client who started at £1,950 is getting strong results and renews, you can hold their rate or move them up at renewal - your call. The principle is simple: as the spaces fill, the price goes up. That is not a sales tactic, it is true. Say it plainly on the call and it lands as honest, not pressured.

