===== content:newsletter (40s, 5321 chars)
The Numbers Beneath the Job is a fortnightly read for MDs and owners in M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting who are winning work but not banking what they expected. Each issue delivers one idea, one example from the trade, and one thing you can do this week.

- **Name:** The Numbers Beneath the Job
- **Who it is for:** MDs and owners of M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting firms turning over £2m to £12m. Founder-led or second-generation businesses where the owner is still the bottleneck.
- **How often:** Fortnightly
- **Format:** Email and LinkedIn newsletter. One idea, one story or example from the trade, one practical action.
- **Call to action:** A 45-minute value session with Zander to look at where the margin is going and what to do about it first.

### The first issue

**Issue 1**

```text
Subject: The Wakefield problem

You finished the job. The lads were busy all summer. No major blowups on site. The variations got submitted. And then the final account came in and the number was nowhere near what you expected.

Most MDs I talk to in M&E and HVAC have a version of that job. Sometimes it's Wakefield. Sometimes it's Sheffield or Doncaster or wherever the big scheme was last year. The job is different. The problem is the same.

Here is what is usually happening.

The margin was already thin at tender. Not because the estimator got it badly wrong, but because the price was built on what things cost last time, not what they cost now. Materials moved. Labour rates crept up. The programme slipped two weeks and nobody repriced the preliminaries.

Then the job ran and the small bleeds added up. A variation that should have been £4,000 got absorbed. The second fix took longer than the programme said. The retention came off the top and nobody flagged that the cash position was tighter than it looked.

Six weeks after the final account lands, you sit down with a spreadsheet and try to work out where it went. By then you cannot change anything. The subbies are on the next job. The MC has moved on. The money is gone.

The fix is not more time after the fact. It is a weekly number during the job.

One figure, updated every week: what we priced, what we have spent, what is left, and whether the margin is holding. That is it. It does not need to be complicated. It just needs to happen every week, not once the final account lands.

At Harlow & Pike, getting a weekly job cost review into the rhythm was one of the first things we did when I took over the HVAC division. The division was doing £4.2m at the time. It was not that the lads were doing bad work. It was that nobody had sight of the numbers until the job was over. Once we had the weekly rhythm in place, problems stopped being surprises. We caught the bleeds early enough to do something about them. Five years later the division was at £11.8m, and the margin held through the growth.

One practical thing you can do this week: pick one live job and ask your contracts manager to give you a single-page cost summary by Friday. Priced value, costs to date, forecast final cost, forecast margin. One page. If it takes more than an hour to pull together, that is your answer on why the final accounts keep coming in short.

If you want to talk through what a weekly numbers rhythm looks like for your business, I have got a 45-minute session where we look at where the margin is going and what to fix first. No pitch. Just the numbers.

Book it here: [value session link]

Zander

P.S. Eight contractors at a time is my limit. If this landed at the right moment, it is worth a conversation sooner rather than later.
```

_Send fortnightly via email and post to LinkedIn newsletter. Tone is direct and personal throughout. No graphics needed for the first issue._

### The next six issues

| Issue | Subject line | The one idea |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 2 | Retention money is not a bonus | Retention cash is earned revenue sitting idle. Most firms have no single person accountable for chasing it on a set schedule. One owner, one list, one weekly check is enough to start moving it. |
| 3 | Why the phone still goes to you | Owner dependency is not a personnel problem. It is a structural one. Until there is a clear weekly rhythm of accountability below the MD, the calls keep coming through to the top, including on days off. |
| 4 | The variation that got absorbed | Variations that are not submitted promptly get absorbed into the job cost and written off informally. A simple register, updated weekly, stops earned money disappearing before the final account. |
| 5 | What planned maintenance did that installs never could | One-off installs produce one invoice. A planned maintenance contract produces twelve. The model shift from project to contract revenue is how you build a business that runs without the MD selling every job. |
| 6 | Six-week-old accounts tell you what already happened | Management accounts that arrive late are history, not intelligence. A weekly job cost number, however rough, gives you sight of a problem when there is still time to fix it. |
| 7 | The job that looked fine at tender | Margin does not disappear at the final account. It bleeds out during the job, in small amounts, on decisions nobody flagged at the time. The only way to catch it is to be looking at the right number every week. |

