### This week

1. **Rewrite the LinkedIn profile.** Lead with the Harlow & Pike proof and the margin bleed problem. Your headline should say what you do and who it is for, in plain contractor language. Your About section should open with the £4.2m to £11.8m result, then describe the three problems you fix, then tell MDs what to do next. No coaching jargon. No generic language. If an M&E MD reads it and does not recognise himself in the first three lines, rewrite it.
2. **Build your first prospect list of 100.** Use LinkedIn search: filter by job title (MD, Owner, Managing Director), industry (building services, HVAC, mechanical and electrical, facilities management), company size (11 to 50 employees), location (West Yorkshire, East Midlands, North West). Add anyone you already know from your Harlow & Pike years. You are not messaging anyone yet. Get the list to 100 qualified names before you send a single connection request.
3. **Set your calendar up and confirm your slots.** Block Tuesdays and Wednesdays 08:00 to 14:00 for client work. Block Mondays and Thursdays from 13:00 for prospect calls. Connect your calendar to the Profit Coach booking page so any MD can book a slot without a back-and-forth. Test the booking link yourself and send it to one trusted contact to confirm it works. Nothing should sit between a conversation and a booked call.

> **Your most likely constraint: conversations stalling before a call gets booked:** Zander's profile and proof are strong, and the sector is right. The point where most coaches in this position lose momentum is the gap between a connection accepting and a call being booked. An MD in a contracting firm is busy, sceptical, and not looking for a coach. If the follow-up message is too long, too salesy, or sounds like it was written for a different industry, he will read it and move on. Watch for this: if your acceptance rate is above 30% but your reply rate is below 8%, the follow-up message is the problem, not the targeting. Keep the message short, specific to the sector, and end with a single low-commitment question, not a pitch and not a booking link on the first contact.
